; broken.mail.test
;
; One fault per record, each chosen so a naive check reports something plausible rather than
; failing. The audit chains must FAIL against this domain, and each failure must name the record
; it came from.
;
; Addresses come from the RFC 5737 documentation range and the domain is under .test, which
; RFC 2606 reserves, so nothing here can route anywhere real.

$ORIGIN broken.mail.test.
$TTL 60

@       IN  SOA  ns1.broken.mail.test. admin.broken.mail.test. (
                 2026092401 ; serial
                 3600       ; refresh
                 900        ; retry
                 604800     ; expire
                 60 )       ; minimum

@       IN  NS   ns1.broken.mail.test.
ns1     IN  A    127.0.0.1

@       IN  A    192.0.2.30

; No MX at all, so this domain cannot receive mail. Deliberately still has an A record, because
; some MTAs fall back to it and an audit should say so rather than reporting the domain dead.

; TWO SPF records, which RFC 7208 makes a permerror rather than "the first one wins". This is the
; fixture that catches a checker written with DNS_ANSWER_VALUE: it takes the first answer, sees a
; syntactically fine -all record, and reports the domain correctly configured. Which of the two a
; resolver returns first is not defined, so a checker built that way is also non deterministic.
@       IN  TXT  "v=spf1 ip4:192.0.2.30 -all"
@       IN  TXT  "v=spf1 include:_spf.example.net ~all"

; A revoked DKIM key. The record EXISTS and parses, and p= is empty, which RFC 6376 defines as the
; key having been withdrawn. A check that only asks whether the selector resolves reports this as
; DKIM being configured.
sel1._domainkey IN TXT  "v=DKIM1; k=rsa; p="

; A DMARC record with two syntax errors: p= names a policy that does not exist, and rua is not a
; URI. A receiver discards the whole record, so the effect is the same as publishing nothing, and
; a check looking only for the string v=DMARC1 reports it as present.
_dmarc  IN  TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=bogus; rua=dmarc@broken.mail.test"

; An MTA-STS TXT record with no policy file behind it. The DNS half advertises the policy and the
; HTTPS fetch 404s, which is worse than not publishing at all: a sending MTA that cached an earlier
; policy keeps enforcing it and cannot refresh.
_mta-sts        IN TXT  "v=STSv1; id=20260924000000"
